3.0535.99An embodiment described within Applicant's specification is the RF based tracker 110 being placed in a room (e.g. at a fixed location), and the user 102 wearing the user equipment 104 is walking around the room while the RF based tracker 110 generates the RF based data used to derive and provide the pseudo-sensor data. Refer to Applicant's filed specification at page 6, lines 18-20: "For example, the user may be in a room, and as the user walks around the room, the RF based tracker may receive RF signal returns from the user. The received RF signals may be decoded and/or digitized into RF based data." This embodiment (where the RF based tracker 110 provides the pseudo-sensor data to a different device, namely the user equipment 104 having the application) is not described by Gillian.

SVG
{EMBED HERE}
3.2775.567Second, in Stivoric the transmitting device transmitting the data fails, whereas in Applicant's case the receiving device receiving the pseudo sensor data is failing to provide native sensor data for itself. Stivoric describes at paragraph [0223] that a sensor or accelerometer may fail, but the failing sensor or accelerometer is located near the temperature module 55 that provides the data to the receiver. Refer for example to Stivoric paragraph [0122] (Adhesive material is in contact with a portion of leaf spring module 230 on first short side 250 and extends to skin of wearer) and paragraph [0123] ("The module being restrained by the adhesive is also exhibits to certain detectable movement, which may act as a shuttle in an accelerometer. This displacement may then provide basic information regarding activity and motion similar to an accelerometer"). Whereas in Applicant's case the opposite is implemented: the receiver (the user equipment 104) fails to provide the native sensor data, which is why the RF based tracker 110 provides the pseudo-sensor data.
